                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "899baf54-7a32-3e77-a771-f17ad12a94d7",
                        "entry_id": "4b350660-3071-3c0a-894e-cd803b7785a2",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The hydrated hydrogen ion, H₃O⁺."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "physical"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "e83eb09d-8070-4824-aa75-cd08c60b8297",
                                "sense_id": "899baf54-7a32-3e77-a771-f17ad12a94d7",
                                "text": "The case of hydronium ions (H₃O⁺) is an example of this. In the gase^([sic]) phase, hydronium is a weak acid. The data in Table 3.10 indicates that bare hydronium ions have a higher electron affinity than most metal surfaces, so a bare hydronium ion should be neutralized when it absorbs on a bare metal surface. However, a hydrated hydronium ion is a stronger acid."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4b00747e-7969-4fbf-b1db-1fdc1c7ec1aa",
                                "sense_id": "899baf54-7a32-3e77-a771-f17ad12a94d7",
                                "text": "Acids are compounds that produce hydronium ions when mixed with water.[…]The hydronium ion is a very special ion because it is the defining particle of all acids."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "2dfa3b49-51b2-4958-964d-8f0ea74be27c",
                                "sense_id": "899baf54-7a32-3e77-a771-f17ad12a94d7",
                                "text": "Hydronium is not a way of stabilizing an acid, because the structure itself is so quickly transient that it can be considered as not existing."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "dbbe6d9a-c64f-4e68-9665-d4a50fc4c989",
                                "sense_id": "899baf54-7a32-3e77-a771-f17ad12a94d7",
                                "text": "There is experimental evidence for the formation of coordination groups of hydroniums with several water molecules by hydrogen bridges."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
